      "id": 1,
      "text": "Mebane takes its name from Brigadier General Alexander Mebane, a North Carolina militia officer who served in Congress during the 1790s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cityofmebanenc.gov/about/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The arrival of the railroad in 1855 sparked Mebane’s growth, leading to its 1881 incorporation as Mebanesville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cityofmebanenc.gov/about/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Mebane Historical Museum curates the town’s cultural heritage through exhibitions, events, and educational programs that celebrate local history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mebanehistoricalmuseum.org/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Several Mebane landmarks, including the Mebane Commercial Historic District and the Old South Mebane Historic District, are listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mebane%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Mebane ranks among North Carolina’s fastest-growing municipalities, reflecting rapid development over recent decades.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mebane%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
